card werk-hmr-swap  [decided]
A hot reload keeps the server and swaps in the new request handler
why: The guard on the global object must keep every long-lived thing, but an agent that edits a route handler also needs to see the edit without a restart.
subtree: 2 cards, 1 open, 1 decided

The guard keeps the listening server, its port, the open live-channel connections and the long-lived children on the global object. Each hot reload evaluates the start-up again. The guard then puts the request handler of the new evaluation in place of the old one and logs that it reuses the live server. It never listens a second time and never starts a second child. So an edit to a route handler is live after the reload, and only an edit to the start-up code itself needs a restart. See #werk-hmr for the guard and the runtime.

